\title{Where the machine's verdict can differ from the official checker's}
\taxon{Design}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{soundness}
\tag{machine}
\tag{reference-engine}
\meta{status}{accepted}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\meta{origin}{docs/design/review/official-divergence.md}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-25}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} accepted. This is the technical record of the classes known on 2026-11-24. Every class is of kind T: the accepted statement is derivable in Lean's type theory, and the official checker rejects it only through its own non-transitivity, cache order or object identity. No class of kind F, an accepted statement that is not derivable, is known. Revised on 2026-11-24 for the state at the end of that day: the machine is the default engine, \code{eager} and the two proof-comparison shortcuts are in its default set, the rejections of official accepts through dropped metadata are closed, and through the launcher of that configuration 68 of the 1,196 rows of the hostile suite are tolerated accepts. Revised on 2026-11-25: one hostile file of class 1 is accepted only since a repeat of an accepted declaration is not checked again, and the suite has 1,274 rows, 78 of them tolerated accepts.}

\p{The policy that tolerates these classes is the [exactness contract](design-exactness-contract), summarised for reviewers in [accepted risks](accepted-risks-divergence). Ground truth for every verdict below is the official checker of Lean v4.36.0 run on the export. The export names are those of the hostile suite and of the reviewers' own sets; see [the gate](design-gate).}

\subtree[design-official-divergence-root]{
\title{The common root}

\p{The official \code{\verb~|is_def_eq~} is not transitive and not a function of the pair it is asked about. The smallest witness is a triple found by the first soundness review:}

\pre{\verb~|
A   := True /\ True      -- intro : A is its constructor application, h : A is an axiom
d x := And.rec (fun _ _ => 5) x

d intro = d h     accepted   same head, the arguments are equal by proof irrelevance
d intro = 5       accepted   the recursor reduces on the constructor
d h     = 5       rejected   the recursor is stuck on the axiom
~}

\p{Two further facts make the answer depend on more than the pair. The cache \code{\verb~|m_whnf_core~} of the reduction without unfolding is read by its cheap calls and written only by its full ones, so the answer for a pair depends on what was reduced earlier in the same declaration; all official caches are keyed by terms and live for one declaration. And one test in \code{\verb~|is_def_eq_core~} is pointer equality, so the answer depends on whether a subterm is one record of the export or two. An engine that computes on values and keys its memo tables by values answers the question of the theory, which is transitive. Why no class below can produce kind F: every step the machine takes is a conversion that holds in the theory, the theory's definitional equality is transitive, and typing is closed under it; a memo that answers yes was written by a yes of the complete procedure.}
}

\subtree[design-official-divergence-standin]{
\title{Class 1: stand-in types of terms the kernel has not reduced}

\p{The official kernel types a bound variable by its binder's declared type, and it infers the type of terms it has not reduced. The machine binds a variable to a value and types values, so it can use a type that is only convertible with the official one. Seven shapes were found, E1-F1 to E1-F7; the official checker rejects every export of the table.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Shape}\th{Export}\th{Machine today}\th{Certificate}}
\tr{\td{F1: a let variable typed by its value}\td{\code{e1-app-type-is-domain-let-bad}}\td{reject, fixed}\td{\code{e1-cert-dh-eq-5-ok}}}
\tr{\td{F2: a redex re-inferred, its variable typed by the argument}\td{\code{e1-redex-function-applied-open-bad}}\td{reject, fixed}\td{the same two steps}}
\tr{\td{F3: a lambda against a Pi, the variable of the other side}\td{\code{e1-lam-fvar-type-in-conv-bad} in v0}\td{reject, fixed}\td{\code{e1-cert-lam-fvar-ok}}}
\tr{\td{F4: the expected-type rule with convertible domains}\td{\code{e1-lam-fvar-type-in-conv-bad} and two siblings}\td{reject; accept with the switch \code{convexpect}, which is off}\td{\code{e1-cert-lam-fvar-ok}}}
\tr{\td{F5: the expected codomain stored as a memo}\td{\code{e1-expect-cod-memo-bad}}\td{reject, fixed}\td{the same two steps}}
\tr{\td{F6: the K rule on an applied let variable}\td{\code{e1-sys-let-krule-major-applied-bad}}\td{declined by a rule}\td{the statement is \code{True}}}
\tr{\td{F7: the type of the structure of a stuck projection, and the raw major premise of the K rule}\td{\code{e1-sib-proj-applied-let-pi-bad}, \code{e1-sib-proj-redex-pi-bad}, \code{e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad}}\td{\strong{accept}; the third only in sets with \code{gated}}\td{the statement is \code{True}}}
}

\p{The certificate \code{e1-cert-dh-eq-5-ok}, which the official checker accepts, proves \code{\verb~|d h = 5~} by \code{Eq.rec} from the two steps the kernel accepts. Exact since the fix: the let rule, the type of a lambda instantiated with an argument of another type (a typed binding), the comparison variable of a binder comparison, and the expected-type memo; see [inference shortcuts and typed bindings](design-inference-shortcuts). The typed bindings cost nothing measurable. Restricting the expected-type rule to one domain value costs 2.4 \% of the ticks of the Cedar slice and 0.3 \% of those of Std, Init and 16 Mathlib slices, and nothing measurable in warm wall clock on the native image with 8 workers: cedar 1.59 and 1.62 s before the fix, 1.59 and 1.58 s after; Std 1.19 and 1.20 s before, 1.19 and 1.22 s after.}

\p{F7 is intrinsic to a machine on values. An exact treatment needs the official type of the unreduced term: the type of a suspension would be the inferred type of its term, a suspension would never be replaced by its known value where it stands for an argument, a stuck projection would keep the suspension of its structure, and the compiled paths would do the same. It was not built. Decline rules instead would cost 322 of 425,620 units of the Mathlib slices and 12 of 100,096 of Std, and would not cover the shapes without a let.}

\p{\strong{F7 with its theorem repeated} (since 2026-11-25). The default configuration no longer checks a definition, theorem or opaque that repeats an accepted earlier one word for word, see [the accept of a repeat](design-verdict-protocol-repeats). On one hostile file that changes the verdict from the official one to a tolerated one:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Export}\th{Official}\th{Default configuration, repeats checked}\th{Default configuration, repeats not checked}\th{Strict policy}\th{Certificate}}
\tr{\td{\code{R-e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad}: the export \code{e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad} of F7 with its theorem appended once more under a new name}\td{reject}\td{reject, at the repeat}\td{\strong{accept}}\td{reject}\td{the statement is \code{True}; the statement of the repeat is the statement of the original}}
}

\p{The machine accepts the original, as the table above says, and rejects the same two records when it checks them again, a rejection that the reference engine then confirms: the proof of the original is a proof block decoded for its unit, the value of the repeat is the promoted copy, a shared term, and the tabling gate of \code{gated} treats the two differently. So before the change the file was rejected at the repeat, which is the official verdict, and \code{\verb~|-X repeats=check~} still gives that. The accept is of kind T: nothing new is accepted, the repeat is the derivable statement of the original with the same proof. It is the only verdict that differs between the two settings on the 1,648 hostile files that were made and judged officially for the question; the file is one of them and not a row of the suite. The maintainer's policy tolerates it, see [the decision of the second wave](dec-RHRE); the evidence is [the report](rep-OPT-I), section 3.5.}
}

\subtree[design-official-divergence-pairs]{
\title{Class 2: a pair table keyed by values}

\p{Exports \code{s3-wc-pos-beta-bad} and \code{s3-wc-canon-fvar-bad}: official reject, machine accept in v0 and in every switch set (finding S3-F1). The machine's table of established comparisons is keyed by values, and its free variables are canonical, so one entry answers a comparison that the official kernel asks again for a new pair of terms: a beta redex, or the same body under another binder instance, after a full reduction changed what the cheap call returns. A third form is a missing memo: when a full reduction continues through a recursor rule into a projection, the projection gets no entry where the official recursion writes one (\code{s3-wc-inner-rec-minor-bad}). The same cause gives a wrong reject of the machine (\code{s3-wc-canon-fvar-full-first-ok}), which the pipeline's re-check repairs. Certificate: the twin of each export with the two sub-proofs in the other order is accepted officially. An exact treatment is the official per-declaration caches mirrored and keyed by terms; a machine on values has no term keys, and the cost was not measured. See [conversion](design-conversion).}
}

\subtree[design-official-divergence-memo]{
\title{Class 3: memo that survives a declaration}

\p{Officially every cache lives for one declaration. A [warm session](design-warm-sessions) keeps the checked type of a term, the positive pairs and the full-reduction memo: exports \code{s3-wc-warm-term-reused-bad} and \code{s3-wc-warm-pair-reused-bad} are accepted under \code{warm} and under the default set, and rejected by v0 (finding S3-F3). The shared check facts of \code{pchk} do the same across declarations and workers: \code{s3-pchk-def-reused-bad} (finding S3-F5). The warm machine alone also rejects a valid file, \code{s3-wc-proj-two-decls-ok}; the strict re-check of the pipeline turns that into the official accept. An exact treatment keeps no memo across declarations that answers a comparison or a cheap reduction; its cost is the gain of \code{warm}, see [the switch reference](design-switches-document).}
}

\subtree[design-official-divergence-switches]{
\title{Class 4: specific to one switch}

\ul{
\li{\code{lazyeval} runs a one-sided unfolding against a weak-head normal side as one reduction, and with it changes which full-reduction memos exist: \code{s3-wc-inner-caseson-lazy-bad} is accepted under \code{lazyeval} alone and under the default set without the case trees; the full default set rejects it (finding S3-F4).}
\li{\code{k2}, the derived rules, gave a wrong accept with its default thresholds (\code{s3-k2-krule-subst-hot-bad}, finding S3-F6): a step of the derivation is decided by a comparison, and the official comparison is not stable under substitution. The switch stays off, see [the decision](dec-MOCU).}
\li{The two [proof-comparison shortcuts](design-proof-comparison-shortcuts) are in the default set since the evening of 2026-11-24, see [the decision](dec-YP0N); when this note was first written they were off. Their review found 15 exports that they accept against the official verdict, and the official checker accepted all 15 statements once the congruence step was written out as a term. The later pass on the candidate default counted 40 such rows in the earlier suites and classified them by family.}
}
}

\subtree[design-official-divergence-reference]{
\title{Class 5: the reference engine and the loader}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Metadata.} The loader drops metadata records, so \code{\verb~|a = mdata b~} reaches the kernel as \code{\verb~|a = b~}; officially the node is a term of its own for every cache. \code{s3-wc-pos-mdata-bad}: official reject, reference engine accept (finding S3-F2). In the other direction \code{s3-mdata-eager-hidden-ok} was accepted officially and rejected by the reference engine, and four more exports were then built that are rejected through another syntactic test of the official kernel and its cache of successful pairs, see [the example](ex-FD6K). A rejection of an official accept is not a tolerated class. It is closed: the loader declines a declaration with a node around an application of \code{eagerReduce}, and the launcher checks a practical rejection of any file with a dropped node again under the strict policy and reports that verdict, see [the decision](dec-7UAL). All of this needs a file with metadata records, which the exporter writes only on request and which no corpus or test has. See also [the decision on metadata](dec-USLM).}
\li{\strong{The let flag.} The \code{nondep} flag of a let is part of the official term identity and is not stored: \code{r2-id-let-nondep-bad}, official reject, reference engine accept (finding R2-F1). No let record of the corpora or the arena tests has the flag set. The strict mode declines a declaration with such a let.}
\li{\strong{Object identity.} \code{r2-ptr-other-record-bad}, official reject, and \code{r2-ptr-same-record-ok}, official accept, are the same terms; they differ in whether one subterm is one export record or two (finding R2-F2). Any engine over a hash-consed store accepts both.}
\li{\strong{Admission.} The official admission of an inductive block runs every judgment in a fresh checker; RLVM keeps the kernel's caches through a phase: \code{r2-adm-param-success-bad} (finding R2-F3, both engines).}
}

\p{The strict mode declines or decides these, see [the caches of the reference engine](design-reference-caches).}
}

\subtree[design-official-divergence-eager]{
\title{Eager reduction: no divergence known}

\p{Without the switch \code{eager} a declaration that enters eager reduction is declined by the machine and decided by the reference engine. With the switch, which is in the default set since the evening of 2026-11-24, the machine decides it with the official verdict or declines; 18 hostile rows are declined by design. See [eager reduction on the machine](design-eager-reduction). This holds for the switch by itself. In the default set it runs together with the two shortcuts, and that combination accepts the hostile rows of the eager family, 19 rows built on one family of equations under eager reduction; the review of the candidate default classed them as kind T by a family argument, without a certificate per row.}
}

\subtree[design-official-divergence-real]{
\title{What the gates show for real inputs}

\p{For the machine with the fix of class 1, on the main tree: the full gate for v0 and for the default switch set with no wrong verdict and no difference; 17,775 mutants for each of the two sets with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0; the differential engine through the launcher on the 230 arena tests, Init, Std, cedar, cslib, reachability, the Cedar slice and 16 Mathlib slices, 1,153,493 engine checks, with 0 verdict differences from the reference engine; seven corpora accepted by the native image with 8 workers. The second pass of the adversarial review, on the candidate default with \code{eager} and the two shortcuts, found no kind F on 987 hostile rows with official verdicts.}

\p{\strong{At the end of 2026-11-24}, with the machine as default engine and that candidate as its default set: the gate of the default configuration through the launcher as a user runs it, judged by official verdicts only, has 230 of 230 published tests correct, 17,775 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0, and on the hostile suite of 1,196 rows 1,124 in agreement, 68 tolerated accepts, 4 declines, no wrong accept and no wrong reject. 83 rows of the suite carry the mark of kind T; the others of them get the official rejection or a decline in this configuration. With the reference engine as default engine the same gate had 6 tolerated accepts on 1,189 rows. The differential engine through the launcher shows no difference on 1,141,195 checks, and all eight corpora are accepted on both launchers with 8 and with 4 workers. Which evidence each tolerated row rests on, and that none of it is a proof, is in [what is left open](prob-HC9J).}

\p{\strong{On 2026-11-25}, after the second optimisation wave: the suite has 1,274 rows, the 78 new ones written against a table of comparison results for closed values that was not adopted (41 official accepts, 37 rejects, 11 of kind T, each with a certificate; see [the report](rep-OPT-H), section 5). The gate of the default configuration through the executable and through the jars, judged by official verdicts: 230 of 230 published tests, 17,775 mutants with SOUNDNESS 0 and COMPLETENESS 0, and on the 1,274 hostile rows 1,192 in agreement, 78 tolerated accepts (the 68 of before and 10 of the new rows, which the default set accepts as it is, without such a table), 4 declines, no wrong accept and no wrong reject. 94 rows carry the mark of kind T. The eight corpora are accepted on both launchers with 8 and with 4 workers, and under the strict policy with no unit declined and no repeat left unchecked. See [the integration of the second wave](rep-INT9).}
}

\p{Detail: [the record of the divergence classes](rep-official-divergence) (the record), [the adversarial review of the default set](rep-soundness-default-set) (classes 2 to 4, the cache-history analysis and the exact options), [the audit of the reference engine's caches](rep-reference-caches) (class 5), [the working notes of package M5](rep-machine-M5) (class 1). Source: [the exactness session](ses-874D) and, for the state at the end of the day, [the finalization session](ses-A14B).}

\p{Findings, examples and open problems: [the non-transitive triple](ex-OTWJ), [the order-dependence pair](ex-YTJ5), [the duplicated projection record](ex-7CCC), [a metadata node](ex-4B5D), [not transitive and not a congruence](ins-4VH2), [not a function of the pair](ins-PO1G), [typing values against typing unreduced terms](ins-AAXM).}
